# Use git bash for every shell command

The workspace default shell is PowerShell, which rejects `&&`, `2>/dev/null`, `$(...)` and
single-quoted Python one-liners. Do not fight it — always invoke git bash instead.

Wrap every command like this:

```
& "C:\Program Files\Git\bin\bash.exe" -lc "cd /c/CodeProjectsLocal/regime-market-agent && <command>"
```

- Use bash syntax inside the quotes: `&&` for chaining, forward slashes for paths, `/c/...` for
  drive roots.
- Escape inner double quotes, or prefer single quotes inside the bash string.
- If a command needs complex quoting (a Python `-c` snippet, a heredoc), write a script file and
  run the file instead of fighting two layers of quoting.

Run Python through the repo venv: `.venv/Scripts/python.exe`, e.g.
`.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pytest tests -q`.

Never use PowerShell-only constructs (`$env:`, `Select-Object`, backtick line continuation), and
never chain with a bare `;` expecting PowerShell to behave like bash.
